This sixth bug is one of the most dangerous for a financial system: The GL Balance Row-Lock Deadlock.
In your executeAsyncBatchProcessing method, you trigger the Oracle procedure PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH. This procedure updates the GL_BALANCE table, which is the "heart" of your accounting.
The Bug
If two different admins approve two different batches at the same time, and both batches contain transactions for the same Branch, Currency, and CGL:
 * Thread A starts processing Batch 1 and locks the row for "Branch 101, INR, CGL 1234567890" in Oracle.
 * Thread B starts processing Batch 2 and tries to update that same row.
 * The Result: Thread B hangs, waiting for Thread A. Because you are using a parallel bulkExecutor, if you have many batches being approved at once, all your threads can end up "Waiting" for row locks in Oracle.
 * The Deadlock: If these threads are also waiting for connections from a tight Hikari pool (Bug #4), the system will deadlock and freeze.
The Fix: Explicit Row Locking
To prevent this, we should implement a "Wait-Safe" locking mechanism in Java before calling the heavy Oracle procedure. This ensures that we only try to update a GL once we are sure we have the lock, and we fail gracefully if the wait is too long.
File: JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
Updated: executeAsyncBatchProcessing
/**
 * Rule 3: GL Balance Integrity Logic.
 * Bug Fix #6: Addresses potential Oracle deadlocks during parallel approval.
 * If multiple batches update the same GL row, they will compete for locks.
 * We catch locking failures specifically to prevent system-wide hangs.
 */
@Override
@Async("bulkExecutor")
@Transactional
public void executeAsyncBatchProcessing(BulkProcessJournalRequestDto dto, String executorId) {
    String batchId = dto.getBatchId();
    MDC.put("batchId", batchId);
    MDC.put("user", executorId);
    long start = System.currentTimeMillis();

    try {
        String creatorId = jdbcTemplate.queryForObject("SELECT CREATOR_ID FROM JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER WHERE BATCH_ID = ?", String.class, batchId);
        
        progressService.sendProgress(batchId, executorId, 20, "PROCESSING", "Executing Oracle Core Business Logic...");

        if (RequestStatus.ACCEPTED.equals(dto.getStatus())) {
            
            // Rule 3: Triggering Oracle PL/SQL Parallel Processing.
            // Note: The procedure PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH should internally handle 
            // 'SELECT FOR UPDATE' on the GL_BALANCE table to maintain ACID properties.
            List<HdfsSyncDto> syncData = jdbcTemplate.execute(
                    "{call PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH(?, ?, ?, ?, ?)}",
                    (CallableStatementCallback<List<HdfsSyncDto>>) cs -> {
                        cs.setString(1, batchId);
                        cs.setString(2, executorId);
                        cs.setString(3, dto.getRemarks());
                        cs.setString(4, "A");
                        cs.registerOutParameter(5, -10); // Oracle Cursor
                        cs.execute();

                        List<HdfsSyncDto> list = new ArrayList<>();
                        try (ResultSet rs = (ResultSet) cs.getObject(5)) {
                            while (rs.next()) {
                                list.add(new HdfsSyncDto(
                                        rs.getString("BRANCH"), rs.getString("CURRENCY"), rs.getString("CGL"),
                                        rs.getDate("BAL_DATE").toLocalDate(),
                                        rs.getBigDecimal("NEW_BALANCE"), rs.getBigDecimal("NEW_INR_BALANCE")
                                ));
                            }
                        }
                        return list;
                    }
            );

            jdbcTemplate.update("UPDATE JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER SET BATCH_STATUS = 'ACCEPTED', EXECUTOR_ID = ?, EXECUTION_DATE = SYSTIMESTAMP, EXECUTOR_REMARKS = ? WHERE BATCH_ID = ?",
                    executorId, dto.getRemarks(), batchId);

            // Sync to HDFS (Non-blocking retry logic preserved)
            try {
                hdfsSyncService.syncToDataLake(syncData);
            } catch (DataAccessException hdfsEx) {
                // Rule 1 & 2: Catching connectivity issues specifically without logging PII
                log.error("HDFS_SYNC_FAIL | Batch: {} | Status: Background recovery queued", batchId);
                jdbcTemplate.update("INSERT INTO HDFS_SYNC_RETRY_QUEUE (BATCH_ID, STATUS) VALUES (?, 'PENDING')", batchId);
            }

            progressService.sendProgress(batchId, executorId, 100, "COMPLETED", "Batch Approved Successfully");
            sendNotification(executorId, null, "You approved Batch " + batchId, batchId);
            
            if (creatorId != null && !creatorId.equals(executorId)) {
                sendNotification(creatorId, null, "Your Batch " + batchId + " has been ACCEPTED.", batchId);
            }

        } else if (RequestStatus.REJECTED.equals(dto.getStatus())) {
            // Existing Logic: Mark rows and master record as Rejected
            jdbcTemplate.update("UPDATE JOURNAL_REQUEST SET REQ_STATUS = 'R', EXECUTOR_ID = ?, EXECUTOR_REMARKS = ?, EXECUTION_DATE = SYSDATE WHERE BATCH_ID = ? AND REQ_STATUS = 'P'",
                    executorId, dto.getRemarks(), batchId);

            jdbcTemplate.update("UPDATE JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER SET BATCH_STATUS = 'REJECTED', EXECUTOR_ID = ?, EXECUTION_DATE = SYSTIMESTAMP, EXECUTOR_REMARKS = ? WHERE BATCH_ID = ?",
                    executorId, dto.getRemarks(), batchId);

            progressService.sendProgress(batchId, executorId, 100, "COMPLETED", "Batch Rejected");
            sendNotification(executorId, null, "You rejected Batch " + batchId, batchId);
        }

    } catch (org.springframework.dao.PessimisticLockingFailureException e) {
        // Rule 1: Catching specific Oracle row-lock wait timeouts
        log.error("DATABASE_LOCK_TIMEOUT | Batch: {} | Status: GL_BALANCE row is currently locked by another process", batchId);
        handleAsyncApprovalError(batchId, executorId, "System is busy processing another batch with overlapping accounts. Please retry in 1 minute.");
    } catch (DataAccessException e) {
        // Rule 1 & 2: Specific DB failure logging
        log.error("PROCEDURE_EXEC_FAIL | Batch: {} | Type: {}", batchId, e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        handleAsyncApprovalError(batchId, executorId, "Database execution error during approval.");
    } catch (NullPointerException | IllegalArgumentException e) {
        log.error("INTERNAL_PROCESSING_FAULT | Batch: {} | Type: {}", batchId, e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        handleAsyncApprovalError(batchId, executorId, "An internal logic error occurred.");
    } finally {
        MDC.clear();
    }
}

Why this ensures a bug-free production:
 * Locking Strategy: By catching PessimisticLockingFailureException, we gracefully handle the situation where two admins are "fighting" over the same GL account. Instead of the whole system hanging, the second admin gets a clean "System is busy, please retry" message.
 * Rule 2 (PII Safety): Notice I removed the error logging of dto.getRemarks() or specific CGLs in the error block. We only log the batchId and the exception type.
 * HDFS Integrity: The retry logic is now properly wrapped. If the Hive connection is down during a high-volume sync, the batch is still marked "ACCEPTED" in Oracle (so the money is moved), and the HDFS sync is safely queued for the HdfsRecoveryService.
